course: dictionary
lesson: 812
title: A word with a dark meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word behead has the word head hidden inside it, and that is your first clue. Context shows the rest: the Queen shouts «Off with their heads!» and threatens to execute everyone. Execute and threat tell you this is about punishment, so behead must mean to cut off a head as a punishment.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Behead that Dormouse!» becomes «Cut off that Dormouse's head as a punishment!», and it still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: Read on for more clues. «You shan't be beheaded!» Alice says, and shan't means shall not. So Alice is saying you must not have your head cut off. The story is not really about cutting off heads: the King quietly pardons everyone.
fact: Behead is a verb: to behead, beheads, beheaded, beheading.
fact: «Behead» means to cut off a person's head as a punishment or an execution.
fact: In history, Anne Boleyn and Charles I were beheaded.
fact: In the French Revolution, the guillotine was used to behead people.
fact: In Alice in Wonderland, the Queen of Hearts shouts «Off with his head!», but the King pardons everyone, so no one is really beheaded.
exercise: «Behead that Dormouse!» What does behead mean? | To wake someone up gently | To put a hat on someone | To tickle someone until they laugh | *To cut off a person's head as a punishment
exercise: Find and copy the word hidden inside behead that names the part of your body on top of your neck. | *head | hear | heat | be
exercise: «You shan't be beheaded!» What does shan't mean? | shell | shower | *shall not | shall
test: In the Alice story, the Queen shouts «Off with their heads!». Is anyone really beheaded? | *No, the King quietly pardons everyone | Yes, Alice is beheaded | Yes, the Queen is beheaded | Yes, the Dormouse is beheaded at once
test: The Queen threatens to execute everyone. What does execute mean here? | To sing loudly | To hide quietly | *To put to death as a punishment | To carry heavy boxes
exam: According to the King's argument, can the Cheshire Cat be beheaded? | *Yes, because anything with a head can be beheaded | No, because a cat has no body to cut from | Only if the Queen says so | Never, because cats are too small
